<!-- #region distances -->

p = (1, 2, 3), q = (4, 6, 3); the differences are ∣pᵢ − qᵢ∣ = 3, 4, 0.

| Metric | Formula | Working | Value |
|---|---|---|---|
| Euclidean (L2) | √Σ(pᵢ − qᵢ)² | √(3² + 4² + 0²) = √25 | **5** |
| Manhattan (L1) | Σ∣pᵢ − qᵢ∣ | 3 + 4 + 0 | **7** |
| Minkowski, r = 3 | (Σ∣pᵢ − qᵢ∣³)^(1/3) | (27 + 64 + 0)^(1/3) = 91^(1/3) | **4.4979** |
| Cosine similarity | p·q / (‖p‖ ‖q‖) | 25 / (√14 × √61) | **0.8555** |

Minkowski with r = 1 is Manhattan and with r = 2 is Euclidean. Cosine is a **similarity** (1 = same direction), not a distance; it ignores length, which is why it suits text.

<!-- #endregion distances -->
<!-- #region knn -->

T-shirt sizes by height (cm) and weight (kg). New customer: **(161, 61)**.

| # | Height | Weight | Size | Distance to (161, 61) | d |
|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|---|--:|
| 1 | 158 | 58 | M | √(9 + 9) = √18 | 4.243 |
| 2 | 158 | 59 | M | √(9 + 4) = √13 | 3.606 |
| 3 | 160 | 59 | M | √(1 + 4) = √5 | 2.236 |
| 4 | 163 | 61 | M | √(4 + 0) = √4 | 2 |
| 5 | 165 | 61 | L | √(16 + 0) = √16 | 4 |
| 6 | 165 | 62 | L | √(16 + 1) = √17 | 4.123 |
| 7 | 168 | 63 | L | √(49 + 4) = √53 | 7.28 |
| 8 | 170 | 64 | L | √(81 + 9) = √90 | 9.487 |

- **k = 3:** nearest are #4, #3, #2 → M 3, L 0 → **M**
- **k = 5:** nearest are #4, #3, #2, #5, #6 → M 3, L 2 → **M**
- **k = 5, weighted** (w = 1/d²): M = 0.527, L = 0.121 → **M**, more decisively, because the M neighbours are closer.

