<!-- #region curse -->

| Pair | Ones shared | Euclidean | Cosine |
|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|
| 111111111110 vs 011111111111 | 10 | 1.4142 | 0.9091 |
| 100000000000 vs 000000000001 | 0 | 1.4142 | 0 |

Euclidean distance calls both pairs equally far apart, although the first pair has 10 ones in common and the second none. Normalising to unit length (cosine) separates them. In high dimensions points become almost equidistant, so "nearest" stops meaning "similar".

<!-- #endregion curse -->
<!-- #region svm -->

A trained linear SVM with **w = (1, 1), b = −3**: the boundary is x₁ + x₂ = 3, the plus-plane x₁ + x₂ = 4, the minus-plane x₁ + x₂ = 2.

| Point | Class | w·x + b | Value | Where it sits |
|:-:|:-:|---|--:|---|
| (3, 1) | +1 | 3 + 1 − 3 | 1 | on the margin: **support vector** |
| (2, 2) | +1 | 2 + 2 − 3 | 1 | on the margin: **support vector** |
| (4, 2) | +1 | 4 + 2 − 3 | 3 | outside the margin |
| (3, 3) | +1 | 3 + 3 − 3 | 3 | outside the margin |
| (1, 1) | −1 | 1 + 1 − 3 | −1 | on the margin: **support vector** |
| (0, 2) | −1 | 0 + 2 − 3 | −1 | on the margin: **support vector** |
| (0, 0) | −1 | 0 + 0 − 3 | −3 | outside the margin |
| (1, 0) | −1 | 1 + 0 − 3 | −2 | outside the margin |

**Margin = 2/‖w‖ = 2/√2 = 1.4142.** Four points touch the margin planes (value ±1): they are the support vectors, and only they decide the boundary. A new point (2.5, 1) gives 2.5 + 1 − 3 = 0.5 > 0 → class **+1** (inside the margin, so the classifier is less sure of it).

